arm64ec: dropping a block from generated code counts as inside the emulator

A block that is checked before it runs (SMCChecks=full, a Mono tail call, and with 0014 code
whose writes cannot be trapped) compares the guest's bytes with the ones it was translated
from, and when they differ it jumps to a handler in the dispatcher that calls
ThreadRemoveCodeEntryFromJit. That drops translated code: it takes the code invalidation
lock, and frees and resets memory (the lookup tables, the thread's call-return stack).

Wine reports a thread's memory calls back to the emulator (NotifyMemoryAlloc, NotifyMemoryFree,
NotifyMemoryProtect) unless the thread is marked as being inside it (InSyscallCallback in the
CPU area). The dispatcher sets the mark around its calls to the compiler and the linker
(EmitSignalGuardedRegion), but not around this one. So the report for the handler's own memory
call arrived while the handler held the code invalidation lock, asked for the lock again, and
the thread waited for itself forever. Any x64 program hung the first time checked code had
changed. The 32-bit (WOW64) build has no such reports and was not affected.

The mark is now set around the call. Found with tests/runtime/code-rewrite-silent.c, which
hung in the case that 0014 answers with a check before each run; it and FEX_SMCCHECKS=full
pass with this.

Local patch only: FEX does not take AI-written code.

diff --git a/FEXCore/Source/Interface/Core/Dispatcher/Dispatcher.cpp b/FEXCore/Source/Interface/Core/Dispatcher/Dispatcher.cpp
--- a/FEXCore/Source/Interface/Core/Dispatcher/Dispatcher.cpp
+++ b/FEXCore/Source/Interface/Core/Dispatcher/Dispatcher.cpp
@@ -689,6 +689,19 @@
     // All dynamic and static registers are spilled coming in to this handler.
     // It's also the end of block and RIP might have changed, so we jump directly to the top of the loop.
     ThreadDispatchRemoveCodeEntry = GetCursorAddress<uint64_t>();
+
+#ifdef ARCHITECTURE_arm64ec
+    // FineWine: the handler drops translated code, which takes the code invalidation lock and frees
+    // and resets memory. Wine reports a thread's memory calls back to the emulator unless the
+    // thread is marked as being inside it, as the calls to the compiler and the linker above are
+    // (EmitSignalGuardedRegion). Without the mark the report arrives while the lock is held and
+    // asks for it again, and the thread waits for itself forever: any block checked before it
+    // runs (SMCChecks=full, a Mono tail call, code whose writes cannot be trapped) hung as soon
+    // as its code changed. TMP3 and TMP4 are free here and are not the arguments.
+    LoadTEBField(TMP4, TEB_CPU_AREA_OFFSET);
+    LoadConstant(ARMEmitter::Size::i32Bit, TMP3, 1);
+    strb(TMP3.W(), TMP4, CPU_AREA_IN_SYSCALL_CALLBACK_OFFSET);
+#endif
 
     // Arguments are already in x0, x1. Just jump to the handler.
     ldr(ARMEmitter::XReg::x2, STATE, offsetof(FEXCore::Core::CpuStateFrame, Pointers.ThreadRemoveCodeEntryFromJIT));
@@ -697,6 +710,11 @@
     } else {
       blr(ARMEmitter::Reg::r2);
     }
+
+#ifdef ARCHITECTURE_arm64ec
+    LoadTEBField(TMP4, TEB_CPU_AREA_OFFSET);
+    strb(ARMEmitter::WReg::zr, TMP4, CPU_AREA_IN_SYSCALL_CALLBACK_OFFSET);
+#endif
 
     FillStaticRegs({
       .OptionalReg = ARMEmitter::Reg::r1,
